Sources written for this project, not carried by any v10 tape.

    nafsmnt.c   mounts a netfs share.  v10 removed gmount -- slot 49 of its
                system call table reads `nosys / was gmount' -- and fmount
                takes an open file descriptor, so the connection is named by
                the fd.  nafsmnt does what mount(8) cannot: tcp_sock(), then
                tcp_connect(), then fmount(1, fd, mount, dev).  fstab(5) has
                five fields and none of them holds a host or a port, so this
                cannot be reached through /etc/mount -a.

    ipnx-v10.m  the kernel configuration: a VAX-11/780 with an RA73 root, 128 MB,
                sixteen MSCP disks, thirty-two DZ11 lines and an Interlan.  The
                file itself says where every number came from.  Written here and
                not in v10/usr/sys/ipnx, because mktape fills /usr/sys by
                extracting secombe and moving src/sys over it -- anything left
                in that tree beforehand is gone -- and nothing copies the
                repository's own v10/usr/sys to a guest.

    streamio.c  the tape's lsys/os/streamio.c plus the four repairs netfs needs:
                a stream head big enough for a reply, an istread that returns on
                a zero count instead of sleeping thirty seconds, one that waits
                for the full count rather than stopping at the first segment,
                and one that keeps the tail of a block the caller could not take.
                PATCHES.md:774-841 argues each.  Carried whole because four edits
                across fifty lines of C is past what a sed can do idempotently.

./patch stages nafsmnt.c into /v10/usr/src/cmd, streamio.c into
/v10/usr/sys/os, and ipnx-v10.m into
/v10/usr/sys/ipnx -- our own site directory, created by ./patch, so that
astro stays the tape's 1127 roster untouched; the mkfile builds the first
into /etc, which is where
/etc/rc calls it from, and the second into /v10/unix.
